## A New Record in Food Recalls

As of today, 2026 has seen an alarming 543 food recalls, continuing a disturbing upward trend in food safety incidents. Out of these, 173 were classified as Class I recalls, the most severe category posing serious health risks. This year alone has seen 86 cases identified as repeat offenders, raising questions about the efficacy of current regulatory measures and the accountability of the companies involved.

## Who's at the Top?

Our top offenders list this year includes familiar names such as Moonlight Packing Corporation, which has already suffered 11 recalls this year, all in the Class I category. Following closely are giants like Albertsons Companies LLC and Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, both with 10 Class I recalls each. Yes, even your favorite chocolate could be on the chopping block.

## What's in Your Food?

Among the reasons for these recalls, allergen issues top the chart with 256 incidents already this year. Combine that with Salmonella contamination in 172 cases and the occasional foreign object (98 cases), and it's a recipe for consumer distrust. Even seemingly minor issues, such as mislabeling--accounting for just four recalls--highlight the meticulous standards required for food safety.

## Recent Incidents

On June 10, 2026, several new recalls were reported. Notable among them:

1. J and J Bunting, Inc recalled their Lunds & Byerly's Curry Powder Sweet for mislabeling an allergenic ingredient.
2. Straus Family Creamery's line of organic ice creams had batches with potential metal contamination.
3. JCB Flavors, LLC issued several Class I recalls due to Salmonella contamination in their White Cheddar Seasoning and other products.
4. A recall of Malazi Tahina by Nassar Investment Company LLC due to its positive test for Salmonella contamination.

## Conclusion

The increasing number of recalls and the return of repeat offenders suggest an urgent need for systemic change in how food safety is monitored and enforced. With over 900 recalls tracked between all time and this year alone accounting for a significant proportion, it's clear that more stringent measures and accountability are necessary.
